shment*"},{"id":"DeuteronomyBookof","title":"Deuteronomy, Book of"},{"id":"Elder","title":"Elder"},{"id":"HammurabiLawCodeof","title":"Hammurabi*, Law Code of"},{"id":"Heart","title":"Heart"},{"id":"JoshuaBookof","title":"Joshua, Book of"},{"id":"Landmark","title":"Landmark*"},{"id":"LawBiblicalConceptof","title":"Law, Biblical Concept of"},{"id":"Neighbor","title":"Neighbor"},{"id":"OffendOffense","title":"Offend, Offense"},{"id":"Saul","title":"Saul"},{"id":"Witness","title":"Witness"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"When the LORD your God has cut off the nations whose land He is giving you, and when you have driven them out and settled in their cities and houses,","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"then you are to set apart for yourselves three cities within the land that the LORD your God is giving you to possess.","verse":2},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"You are to build roads for yourselves and divide into three regions the land that the LORD your God is giving you as an inheritance, so that any manslayer can flee to these cities.","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"And if the LORD your God enlarges your territory, as He swore to your fathers, and gives you all the land He promised them,","verse":8},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"and if you carefully keep all these commandments I am giving you today, loving the LORD your God and walking in His ways at all times, then you are to add three more cities to these three.","verse":9},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Thus innocent blood will not be shed in the land that the LORD your God is giving you as an inheritance, so that you will not be guilty of bloodshed.","verse":10},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"You must not move your neighbor’s boundary marker, which was set up by your ancestors to mark the inheritance you shall receive in the land that the LORD your God is giving you to possess.","verse":14},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"both parties to the dispute must stand in the presence of the LORD, before the priests and judges who are in office at that time.","verse":17}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["The Cities of Refuge"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["The cities of refuge were built to protect innocent people from blood revenge. An avenger had the legal right to put a murderer to death (",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.19","r":"Num.35.19","rawR":"Num.35.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.19","value":"Num.35.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:19"},"; see ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.9.6","r":"Gen.9.6","rawR":"Gen.9.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.9.6","value":"Gen.9.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 9:6"},"). If a person caused someone’s death accidentally, the slayer fled to a city of refuge to find temporary safety while awaiting trial (",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.22-28","r":"Num.35.22-28","rawR":"Num.35.22-28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.22-28","value":"Num.35.22-28"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:22-28"},"). The six cities of refuge were distributed across Hebrew territory so that any Israelite could seek asylum."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["If, after trial, the fugitive was convicted of murder (instead of involuntary manslaughter), he was turned over to the avenger and received his due punishment (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.12","r":"Deut.19.12","rawR":"Deut.19.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.12","value":"Deut.19.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 19:12"},"). If he was found innocent of murder, the slayer remained in the city of refuge, where he was granted asylum. When the high priest died, the slayer was free to return home and was no longer subject to blood revenge. This merciful legal arrangement protected the land from further pollution by innocent blood (see ",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.33","r":"Num.35.33","rawR":"Num.35.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.33","value":"Num.35.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:33"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["It was not acceptable to pay a fine to atone for the death of another human being, even if that death was not premeditated (",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.31-33","r":"Num.35.31-33","rawR":"Num.35.31-33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.31-33","value":"Num.35.31-33"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:31-33"},"). Human life was too precious to be ransomed with a simple payment. When a human being was killed, whether deliberately or by accident, it resulted in alienation or exile."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Atonement does not come easily, whether in ancient Israelite times or in our own day; each person is in need of atonement to restore their relationship with God (see ",{"href":"?bref=Rom.3.23-26","r":"Rom.3.23-26","rawR":"Rom.3.23-26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rom.3.23-26","value":"Rom.3.23-26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rom 3:23-26"},"). Jesus, the Son of God, provides this reconciliation through his work as the Great High Priest who offered himself as the ultimate sacrifice (see ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.4.14-10.18","r":"Heb.4.14-10.18","rawR":"Heb.4.14-10.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.4.14-10.18","value":"Heb.4.14-10.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 4:14–10:18"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.35.6-34","r":"Num.35.6-34","rawR":"Num.35.6-34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.6-34","value":"Num.35.6-34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:6-34"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.1-13","r":"Deut.19.1-13","rawR":"Deut.19.1-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.1-13","value":"Deut.19.1-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 19:1-13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.20.1-9","r":"Josh.20.1-9","rawR":"Josh.20.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.20.1-9","value":"Josh.20.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 20:1-9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.21.13","r":"Josh.21.13","rawR":"Josh.21.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.21.13","value":"Josh.21.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:13"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.21.21","r":"Josh.21.21","rawR":"Josh.21.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.21.21","value":"Josh.21.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"21"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.21.27","r":"Josh.21.27","rawR":"Josh.21.27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.21.27","value":"Josh.21.27"}],"t":"ref","v":"27"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.21.32","r":"Josh.21.32","rawR":"Josh.21.32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.21.32","value":"Josh.21.32"}],"t":"ref","v":"32"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.21.38","r":"Josh.21.38","rawR":"Josh.21.38","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.21.38","value":"Josh.21.38"}],"t":"ref","v":"38"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.1.50-53","r":"1Kgs.1.50-53","rawR":"1Kgs.1.50-53","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.1.50-53","value":"1Kgs.1.50-53"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 1:50-53"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.2.28-34","r":"1Kgs.2.28-34","rawR":"1Kgs.2.28-34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.2.28-34","value":"1Kgs.2.28-34"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:28-34"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:TheCitiesOfRefuge","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"TheCitiesOfRefuge","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c14adef2edce090151c3838f0b706814401972a91d8c1b4e606cab62bdce05f2","sourceItemName":"TheCitiesOfRefuge","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":47,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.1-13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"The Cities of Refuge","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"The Cities of Refuge","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.1-13","r":"Deut.19.1-13","rawR":"Deut.19.1-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.1-13","value":"Deut.19.1-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:1-13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Cities of refuge"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.2","r":"Deut.19.2","rawR":"Deut.19.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.2","value":"Deut.19.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:2"},") were strategically located throughout the land so those accused of homicide could find protective sanctuary until their cases came to trial (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.4.41","r":"Deut.4.41","rawR":"Deut.4.41","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.4.41","value":"Deut.4.41"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:41"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.6-29","r":"Num.35.6-29","rawR":"Num.35.6-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.6-29","value":"Num.35.6-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:6-29"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.1-13","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.1-13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"bc6f90b629327158da8e62fc4c4a3d2cf82e46bc5b0aa34432c86885169c705f","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.1-13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2267,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.1-13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.1-13","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.2","r":"Deut.19.2","rawR":"Deut.19.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.2","value":"Deut.19.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["three cities"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," were those in Canaan, west of the Jordan; there were three more in Transjordan (see ",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.13-14","r":"Num.35.13-14","rawR":"Num.35.13-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.13-14","value":"Num.35.13-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:13-14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.20.7","r":"Josh.20.7","rawR":"Josh.20.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.20.7","value":"Josh.20.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 20:7"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.2","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3c2834115f0163cd413adcc375a7add35ad655b7215bbf3878799694d08a9fc2","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2268,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.4","r":"Deut.19.4","rawR":"Deut.19.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.4","value":"Deut.19.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," A ",{"c":["slayer"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," who ",{"c":["kills . . . unintentionally"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," does not plan ahead of time to commit the act (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.17.8","r":"Deut.17.8","rawR":"Deut.17.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.17.8","value":"Deut.17.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"17:8"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.4","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"4dbe4275d06fcfc8792f837a9807a185d193a583ab488b020d9a32a16e007cd0","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2269,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.6","r":"Deut.19.6","rawR":"Deut.19.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.6","value":"Deut.19.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["avenger"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["the redeemer of blood"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): The law allowed murder to be avenged by members of the victim’s family. When they found the killer, they could put him to death (",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.16-21","r":"Num.35.16-21","rawR":"Num.35.16-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.16-21","value":"Num.35.16-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:16-21"},"). The idea was that blood shed by the victim must be paid for by the blood of the person responsible for the act (see ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.4.15","r":"Gen.4.15","rawR":"Gen.4.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.4.15","value":"Gen.4.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 4:15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.9.6","r":"Gen.9.6","rawR":"Gen.9.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.9.6","value":"Gen.9.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:6"},"). In this way, the land polluted by the shed blood could be purified (",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.33-34","r":"Num.35.33-34","rawR":"Num.35.33-34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.33-34","value":"Num.35.33-34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:33-34"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.6","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b58c4a39c754affb70886931971c139211092fb370b538233b39bb0272116320","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2270,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.9","r":"Deut.19.9","rawR":"Deut.19.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.9","value":"Deut.19.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," If Israel proved faithful to the covenant (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.8","r":"Deut.19.8","rawR":"Deut.19.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.8","value":"Deut.19.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:8"},"), God would grant the nation even more territory, so much so that ",{"c":["three additional cities of refuge"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," would be needed. Sadly, this need never arose because of Israel’s disobedience to the Lord."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.9","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8e32a910f541ca782114f84d545cd9e636b378d6a9b1d18391c44dd6c27b2253","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2271,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.11","r":"Deut.19.11","rawR":"Deut.19.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.11","value":"Deut.19.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The Hebrew term rendered ",{"c":["is hostile"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["is a hater"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},") indicates a constant and long-standing hateful attitude, which constituted evidence that the killer was predisposed to commit a crime and therefore probably did it."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.11","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e91bce7f45f6afc6591020eafb463b7b995c465f1c89b8085afea62a5760a780","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2272,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.13","r":"Deut.19.13","rawR":"Deut.19.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.13","value":"Deut.19.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Purge"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["burn"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): The only way for the land to be free of culpability in matters of homicide was for blood vengeance to be enacted, which would burn out the impurity. See also ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.13.5","r":"Deut.13.5","rawR":"Deut.13.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.13.5","value":"Deut.13.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"13:5"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.17.12","r":"Deut.17.12","rawR":"Deut.17.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.17.12","value":"Deut.17.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"17:12"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.13","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"f8f272eb1a5ce9ada2035a69dadf308239f887d0fd5749de2f5309d2b310ed9f","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2273,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.13","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.15","r":"Deut.19.15","rawR":"Deut.19.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.15","value":"Deut.19.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:15"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The word of one witness alone would count no more than the word of the accused. However, ",{"c":["two or three witnesses"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," would confirm or discount each other’s testimony. For New Testament references to this law, see ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.18.15-20","r":"Matt.18.15-20","rawR":"Matt.18.15-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.18.15-20","value":"Matt.18.15-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 18:15-20"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.8.17-18","r":"John.8.17-18","rawR":"John.8.17-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.8.17-18","value":"John.8.17-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"John 8:17-18"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Cor.13.1-2","r":"2Cor.13.1-2","rawR":"2Cor.13.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Cor.13.1-2","value":"2Cor.13.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Cor 13:1-2"}," and ",{"href":"?item=IICor.13.1-2_StudyNote_Filament","id":"IICor.13.1-2_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"IICor.13.1-2_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.15","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":15,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":15}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.15","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"baf875c22b9d6ba87dc7ef023b2bf1d9d7738960f2ccfe126ef4536a4ee21110","sourceItemName":"Deut.19.15","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2274,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.19.15","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.19.15","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.21","r":"Deut.19.21","rawR":"Deut.19.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.21","value":"Deut.19.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:21"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The legal principle described as ",{"c":["life for life"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is known as ",{"c":["lex talionis"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (“law of retaliation”), according to which the punishment must fit the crime (see ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.21.23","r":"Exod.21.23","rawR":"Exod.21.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.21.23","value":"Exod.21.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 21:23"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.24.20","r":"Lev.24.20","rawR":"Lev.24.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.24.20","value":"Lev.24.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 24:20"},"); it should be neither too lenient nor too severe. Appropriate punishment is a necessary function of government (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Rom.13.1-7","r":"Rom.13.1-7","rawR":"Rom.13.1-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rom.13.1-7","value":"Rom.13.1-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rom 13:1-7"},"), and it served to maintain Israel’s spiritual integrity as God’s people (see ",{"href":"?item=Deut.17.7_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Deut.17.7_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Deut.17.7_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on Deut 17:7"},"). But vengeance belongs to God, not individuals (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.25.26","r":"1Sam.25.26","rawR":"1Sam.25.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.25.26","value":"1Sam.25.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 25:26"},", ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.25.33","r":"1Sam.25.33","rawR":"1Sam.25.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.25.33","value":"1Sam.25.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"33"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.5.38-39","r":"Matt.5.38-39","rawR":"Matt.5.38-39","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.5.38-39","value":"Matt.5.38-39"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 5:38-39"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Cor.6.1-8","r":"1Cor.6.1-8","rawR":"1Cor.6.1-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Cor.6.1-8","value":"1Cor.6.1-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Cor 6:1-8"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.19.21","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":19,"endVerse":21,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":21}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.19.21","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"1f22b2a817b78f263f1c60043643bfa7ee6